A||N||2||roadrunner||Idle speed too fast||||1||
Z||000000||roadrunner||01-19-2001||11:07 PM||||My '86 GT V-6 with 4 speed idles at 2000 rpm upon cold start up and idles at 1000 rpm once the engine is fully warm.  I have checked for vacuum leaks and find none.  I have also change the IAC valve with a known good one with no difference.  My '87 GT automatic idles at 1400 rpm cold and goes to 750-800 when warm in gear.  Any suggestions on the '86 high idle?  Temperature sensor?  What? Thanks.....||||reg||1
Z||000001||Bob Englert||01-19-2001||11:30 PM||||The auto will idle lower when in gear compared to when it is in neutral.  The manual trans car will normally idle at or near 900, but if it isn't giving you any IAC codes, I wouldn't worry too much about it.<P>You might want to check the vac lines for any leaks that could be bumping the idle up.<BR><P>------------------<BR>Bob<BR>2 - 87 GT's<BR>85 SE 4 cyl||||reg||1
Z||000002||mrfiero||01-19-2001||11:55 PM||||Look closely at the EGR pipe......it's likely broken or cracked.  This will cause a high idle and a code 35.  Take some WD-40 and (with car running) spray around the ends of the pipe. If the idle changes you have a leaky pipe.<P>I just went through this on my '88 GT.  I got the code 35 and high idle (1500 RPM).  I replaced he IAC beacuse I cheked the EGR pipe and thought it was good.  I was tinkering with it today and discovered the pipe was completely broken in two!  <P>Other than that, you may want to check the timing.  This can affect the idle speed too. <p>[This message has been edited by mrfiero (edited 01-19-2001).]||||reg||1
